</ul><h1>Maybe Good!</h1><p>It’s time for an exciting round of:</p>
<p><strong>Unheard-of Snack Roulette</strong>!</p>
<p>Or would it be “Unheard-of Snack <em>Russian</em> Roulette”? After all, it’s more like that one than the one with the balls and the wheel and whatnot.</p>
<p>Basically, what we have here are some snacks from a brand you’ve very likely never heard of, being sold at volume for a price that’s pretty cheap. In other words, a high-ceiling, low-floor proposition. You like them? Awesome. You’ve got plenty. You hate them? Yikes. Maybe you didn’t break the bank, but that’s a lot of bags to unload.</p>
<p>So, what will it be? Are you gonna buy them or not?</p>
<p>What’s that? You’d like more information before you make your decision?</p>
<p>Well, too friggin’ bad, buddy! Because there’s barely any to share!</p>
<p>For example, head over to the Amazon page, and you’ll find that these get a respectable 4.4 out of 5… based on an outright shameful 14 reviews. You can’t trust that at all. Because you just know, without even reading them, that a bunch of five-star reviews will be like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I found this product useful/beautiful/delicious (choose one)! Would recommend!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And then most of the one-star reviews, will be like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I ordered these to be delivered to my front door in Phoenix on August 14th, the day after I left for a three-week vacation. I was looking forward to trying them when I got home. But, unfortunately, they were delivered defective (melted). I hope to receive a full refund. Amazon! Issue the refund! Hello? Is anyone there?!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then there’s the product’s own marketing. They’re vegan. They’re nut-free. They’re gluten-free. They’re plant-based. They’ve got “less sugar.” It’s all cool, sure, but what does it mean about the <em>flavor</em>? Are they emphasizing all these aspects to divert attention from their off-putting taste? Or is this like <a href="https://meh.com/deals/urbanista-phoenix-hybrid-active-noise-cancelling-earbuds-with-solar-charging-zew">those solar-charging buds</a> we sold recently, where the marketing team got out over their skis talking about the case they come in and forgot to mention that they sound good?</p>
<p>The verdict, at least according to Meh customers, the last time we sold these (when we offered a few different flavors) was… mixed.</p>
<p><a href="/@fanofuna">@fanofuna</a> said:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These taste gross. Too bad I can’t return.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To which <a href="/@pakopako">@pakopako</a> replied:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I find they’re kind of OK. At least the mint chocolate I got (which was mostly mint). What flavor did you get?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And <a href="/@Trinityscrew">@Trinityscrew</a> said:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These things are horrible. I won’t contact customer service because I don’t believe they’re defective. They’re just awful.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To which pakopako again replied:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I still don’t understand why people find these <em>horrible</em>. Did I get a different flavor?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Speaking of marketing strategy, is this a smart one? Including reviews of people who are calling the product “gross” and “horrible” and “awful”? Probably not. But I know from experience that this does create a bit of morbid curiosity around a product that can be intriguing.</p>
<p>(For me, it was with some electrolyte packets that the community deemed totally nasty and vomit-inducing. I just had to try them, so I bought some, and my reaction was much like pakopako’s; I didn’t think they were amazing, but they definitely weren’t terrible.)</p>
<p>Which side will you be on? Trinityscrew and fanofuna’s? Or pakopako’s? Or might you [gasp] like them even more?!</p>
<p>Only one way to find out.</p>